---
description: Solarian coding standards - no thin wrappers, no dead defensiveness, no narrating comments
alwaysApply: true
---

# Code style

## No single-use wrapper methods

A method that only forwards to one call, at one call site, is boilerplate. Inline it.
A helper earns its name by having several callers or by naming a non-obvious concept.

```python
# BAD
def on_start(self):
    self._start_teleop()

def _start_teleop(self) -> None:
    self.start_skill(CartesianTeleopSkill(arm=ARM, gripper=GRIPPER))

# GOOD
def on_start(self):
    self.start_skill(CartesianTeleopSkill(arm=ARM, gripper=GRIPPER))
```

Also delete: methods with zero call sites, and overrides whose body is `pass` when the
base hook already does nothing.

## No defensive reads of attributes `__init__` always sets

`getattr(self, "x", default)` and `(d or {}).get(k) or ""` say "this might not exist".
When it always exists, that is a lie that hides the real optional cases. Fix the
initialisation order if that is what made the guard necessary, then read directly.

```python
# BAD
self._local_skills.pop(getattr(skill, "skill_name", ""), None)
name = str((action.data or {}).get("skill") or "")

# GOOD
self._local_skills.pop(skill.skill_name, None)
name = str(action.data.get("skill") or "")
```

Keep the defensiveness where it is real: payloads deserialized off the wire, genuinely
optional config, and polymorphic attributes only some subclasses declare. Say why in a
comment when it is not obvious.

## Use imported classes directly

Do not alias an imported class onto `self`. `self._arm_action_cls = RightArmAction`
adds indirection and hides which class runs. Call `RightArmAction(...)`.

## Comments state constraints, never narrate

A comment earns its line by saying something the code cannot: a unit, an invariant, a
reason this order matters, a trade-off. Never restate what the next line does, and never
explain a change for a reviewer — that belongs in the commit message.
